  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nOnce again, no significant change to our weather is expected \nthrough the weekend. The fog setup tonight is a lower confidence\nelement. The inland extent of modestly enhanced low level\nmoisture behind the lake breeze passage this afternoon may\ncontinue to be offset by deep mixing and recent dryness. In\naddition, forecast soundings, even on guidance that has been\nsimulating fog development over northeast Illinois, indicate\n10-15 kt south-southeast winds just above a very shallow moist\nlayer late tonight. These above elements may serve to somewhat\ncounteract more appreciable fog development despite an otherwise\nfavorable setup with ideal radiational cooling conditions. \n\nOur official forecast indicates patchy fog initially in far \nnortheast Illinois earlier in the overnight. Then into the early\nmorning hours, we're advertising the potential for shallow \npatchy ground fog down into the Chicago metro (away from \ndowntown) and far northwest Indiana. Overall, confidence is on\nthe lower side, also considering the lack of stronger support\nfrom statistical (MOS) guidance for areas of dense fog. A\nrelatively higher chance for patchy dense fog anywhere in our\nCWA still likely is confined to primarily within Lake County IL\n(roughly in the 3 AM to 8 AM Friday timeframe). \n\nFriday looks to be a near carbon copy of today both in terms of\nthe lake breeze along the Illinois shore and temps. Southeasterly\nsynoptic winds on the back side of the surface high pressure \nshould keep the lake breeze confined to close to the immediate \nnorthwest Indiana shoreline. \n\nThis weekend, a strong trough is progged to track east \nacross central Canada with a trailing cold front pushing east \nacross the northern Plains toward the upper Mississippi Valley. \nThis should result in more of a southerly then southwesterly \ngradient, albeit still not that strong, to develop this weekend.\nWhile not strong, it will probably be enough to hamper lake \nbreeze development and result in some slightly warmer temps \nadvecting into the area. Temperatures over the weekend could \nclimb into the mid to perhaps locally upper 70s on Sunday given \nenough sunshine. \n\nAs the strong upper ridge and omega block start breaking down,\nit will probably allow that cold front to move gradually \nsouthward across the area Monday as it lays out east-west. \nThere is still spread in guidance on timing of the frontal \npassage and given it will be running into the weakening upper \nridge, it shouldn't have much push or a significant cold punch \nbehind it. It should be enough to start cooling our temps down \ncloser to, but possibly still slightly above, average by Tuesday\nand Wednesday. At some point early-middle next week, shortwave \nenergy from the western trough could meander east into the \nMidwest bringing the threat for some rain to the area. Given the\ntendency for medium range guidance to struggle with breaking \ndown blocking patterns, it isn't surprising that there is \nconsiderable spread in how that plays out and when/if it will \nrain here next week. \n\nCastro/Izzi",
